WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.880
SAAD ALI: Hello, everyone.

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:01.713
My name is Saad Ali.

00:00:01.713 --> 00:00:04.290
I'm a software engineer at
Google here at KubeCon Seattle.

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:05.714
And I have with me Dan Ciruli.

00:00:05.714 --> 00:00:06.630
DAN CIRULI: Nailed it.

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:07.697
Good job, Saad.

00:00:07.697 --> 00:00:08.530
SAAD ALI: Thank you.

00:00:08.530 --> 00:00:10.090
I've been practicing that.

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:12.124
Dan, you are a product
manager of Istio.

00:00:12.124 --> 00:00:13.290
DAN CIRULI: This is correct.

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:14.706
SAAD ALI: And we
have been talking

00:00:14.706 --> 00:00:16.290
to a few folks about Istio.

00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:19.115
Can you talk a little bit about
why is Istio so important?

00:00:19.115 --> 00:00:21.240
Why should someone care,
or why should they use it?

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:23.610
DAN CIRULI: Well, I think
in general people should

00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:26.940
be using Istio
because the fact is

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:28.770
people are deploying
applications now

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:29.850
that are complex.

00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:33.120
And there's lots of
communication between pods,

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:34.452
between clusters even.

00:00:34.452 --> 00:00:36.910
And when there's communication
as part of your application,

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:39.600
it's really important to be
able to understand what's

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:42.650
communicating with what, control
what's communicating with what,

00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:43.680
and secure all that.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:45.330
So applications are
distributed now.

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:47.040
You need a way to
reason about that,

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:48.090
to secure it and
then control it.

00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:50.215
SAAD ALI: And Istio provides
a nice way to do that.

00:00:50.215 --> 00:00:52.470
It builds on top of
Kubernetes, and you

00:00:52.470 --> 00:00:54.420
don't have to have
that logic hardcoded

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:56.910
into every single
application that you write.

00:00:56.910 --> 00:00:58.959
And you can make modifications
at a fleet level.

00:00:58.959 --> 00:01:00.000
DAN CIRULI: That's right.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.455
Right now typically
people who either

00:01:02.455 --> 00:01:04.080
don't secure their
applications or they

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:07.290
spend a lot of developer
time figuring out

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:09.450
how to do authentication
and authorization,

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:11.610
same thing goes for monitoring.

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:13.214
Same thing goes for
traffic control.

00:01:13.214 --> 00:01:14.880
And if your developers
are writing, say,

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:17.185
authentication code, they're
not writing business code.

00:01:17.185 --> 00:01:18.810
If you're an insurance
company, they're

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:20.220
not writing insurance app code.

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:22.770
And so what we try to do is
just give more out of the box

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:25.785
so developers can spend more
of their time writing services.

00:01:25.785 --> 00:01:28.470
SAAD ALI: So that's the classic
separation of concerns design

00:01:28.470 --> 00:01:30.340
principle being applied
at a larger scale.

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:31.140
DAN CIRULI: That's
exactly right.

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:31.500
SAAD ALI: Great.

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:32.880
So can you talk
a little bit more

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:36.060
about what the current
functionality is in terms

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:37.722
of the open source product?

00:01:37.722 --> 00:01:38.430
DAN CIRULI: Yeah.

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:41.250
So the three main things that
people are getting from Istio

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:44.070
today are observability,
which is just

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:47.310
the ability to understand how
your application is working.

00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:49.950
That means gathering
telemetry on every call.

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:54.150
It means gathering logs
on every call and traces

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:55.860
and putting those
in a common place.

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:58.092
In open source, that
might be Prometheus.

00:01:58.092 --> 00:02:00.300
Some people are using it to
send all that information

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:01.140
to Datadog.

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:04.010
We have an open adapter that's
sending it to Stackdriver.

00:02:04.010 --> 00:02:06.840
The second big thing is control.

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:08.850
When a service needs
a call somewhere else,

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:09.940
where is that service?

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:10.789
How do you find it?

00:02:10.789 --> 00:02:13.830
Is there say a canary
rollout going right now?

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:15.549
Istio let's you control that.

00:02:15.549 --> 00:02:17.340
And then the last thing
really is security,

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:19.730
automatically putting
MTLS on every single call

00:02:19.730 --> 00:02:22.320
so that all of your data
is encrypted in transit

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:24.930
and so that once you have a
strong identity for each call,

00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:26.220
you can perform authorization.

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:28.470
There's three big benefits
that they're getting today.

00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:29.250
SAAD ALI: That sounds awesome.

00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:31.500
And what's coming up next
in terms of the open source

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:32.124
side of things?

00:02:32.124 --> 00:02:33.930
DAN CIRULI: In terms
of open source,

00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:37.050
we've got a big focus on
scalability and performance.

00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:40.572
We've got people who are
running in production today.

00:02:40.572 --> 00:02:42.030
We also have people
who are not yet

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:44.040
running in production
because they're too big,

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:46.440
people who are running
thousands of services.

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:48.340
Much like Kubernetes
in the early days,

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:49.630
Istio wasn't ready for that.

00:02:49.630 --> 00:02:54.066
So we've had a big emphasis on
scalability and performance.

00:02:54.066 --> 00:02:56.190
Second big thing we're
working on is multi-cluster.

00:02:56.190 --> 00:02:59.102
Lots of people are running
multiple clusters today.

00:02:59.102 --> 00:03:01.560
It might be that they're doing
it across different regions.

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:03.942
It might be that they need
for data locality reasons,

00:03:03.942 --> 00:03:06.150
and it might be because
they're using different cloud

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:07.750
providers and/or data centers.

00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:10.020
But in all those cases,
they need communication

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:12.480
between those clusters,
and so we are working hard

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:15.750
to make it much easier to
simultaneously make calls

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:18.759
from cluster to cluster while
maintaining that observability,

00:03:18.759 --> 00:03:19.675
security, and control.

00:03:19.675 --> 00:03:20.440
SAAD ALI: Got it.

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:22.800
So definitely a lot more
coming down the pipe.

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:25.689
I understand that GKE offers
Istio as a managed service.

00:03:25.689 --> 00:03:28.230
Can you talk a little bit about
that and what GKE adds to it?

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:29.896
DAN CIRULI: So what
we announced today--

00:03:29.896 --> 00:03:33.180
yesterday was Istio on GKE.

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:35.790
And what that is it's an
automated installation

00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:40.405
and upgrade of the Istio
components into a GKE cluster.

00:03:40.405 --> 00:03:42.530
What that means is that
some people have complained

00:03:42.530 --> 00:03:43.740
that helm is relatively new.

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:45.030
People don't really like it.

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:47.275
We said, hey, we'll do
the installation for you.

00:03:47.275 --> 00:03:48.960
It helps people get started.

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:51.870
More importantly, we do
the upgrade for you, too.

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:52.620
Upgrades are pain.

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:54.690
One of the things
people love about GKE

00:03:54.690 --> 00:03:56.730
is that we upgrade
your nodes for you.

00:03:56.730 --> 00:03:57.570
We update GKE.

00:03:57.570 --> 00:03:59.520
Well, if you're
using Istio and GKE,

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:02.494
we also update the
Istio components.

00:04:02.494 --> 00:04:03.660
And so that's a big benefit.

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:05.701
It's just going to make
it easier to get started.

00:04:05.701 --> 00:04:07.950
We also are by default
sending all your metrics,

00:04:07.950 --> 00:04:10.980
all your logs, and
traces to Stackdriver.

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:14.700
So people can go create really
good service dashboards,

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:16.470
the golden signals
that Istio monitors.

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:19.380
What is the rate of traffic
on each service, what's

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:21.120
the error rate, what
are your latencies

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:22.250
and different percentiles?

00:04:22.250 --> 00:04:23.800
Lots of people don't know that.

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:25.350
If you use Istio
on GKE, you're just

00:04:25.350 --> 00:04:26.570
going to see that
in Stackdriver.

00:04:26.570 --> 00:04:28.403
SAAD ALI: And so what
is your recommendation

00:04:28.403 --> 00:04:30.831
for customers who might be
interested in adopting Istio?

00:04:30.831 --> 00:04:31.830
How do they get started?

00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:34.480
DAN CIRULI: Oh, I think this is
the easiest way to get started.

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:37.770
Turn up a GKE cluster or take
an existing one, install it.

00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:41.400
One thing to note is that that
for Istio, some of the things

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:43.830
it does fairly transparently,
if you're just using it

00:04:43.830 --> 00:04:46.270
for monitoring, you don't
have to change the way you

00:04:46.270 --> 00:04:47.520
write your application at all.

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:49.530
Same thing is true for MTLS.

00:04:49.530 --> 00:04:51.880
If you want to get into
more sophisticated features,

00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:53.750
if you want to try
doing canary rollouts,

00:04:53.750 --> 00:04:56.250
say, you're going to have to
understand the Istio networking

00:04:56.250 --> 00:04:58.374
concepts, and you have to
think about those things.

00:04:58.374 --> 00:05:00.010
But if what you
really want is just

00:05:00.010 --> 00:05:03.094
observability into your services
or just MTLS in your services,

00:05:03.094 --> 00:05:04.510
you don't have to
change anything.

00:05:04.510 --> 00:05:06.176
Install it and it'll
just start working.

00:05:06.176 --> 00:05:07.990
SAAD ALI: That's super cool.

00:05:07.990 --> 00:05:09.560
Let's switch gears a little bit.

00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:11.270
And we're here at
KubeCon, of course.

00:05:11.270 --> 00:05:14.092
This is the largest
KubeCon we've ever had.

00:05:14.092 --> 00:05:15.800
I imagine this is not
your first KubeCon.

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:16.620
DAN CIRULI: It is not.

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:17.920
SAAD ALI: Can you
tell us a little bit

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:18.970
about what the
changes that you've

00:05:18.970 --> 00:05:21.400
seen over time through with
the various KubeCons you've

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:24.140
attended and the
community at large.

00:05:24.140 --> 00:05:26.920
DAN CIRULI: This is
an amazing conference.

00:05:26.920 --> 00:05:29.020
I've been to a lot of
conferences in my day.

00:05:29.020 --> 00:05:34.660
And the sense of community
here and the energy is bizarre.

00:05:34.660 --> 00:05:35.430
It really is.

00:05:35.430 --> 00:05:37.230
And I'll tell you,
I've been a vendor.

00:05:37.230 --> 00:05:39.040
We're on a show floor right now.

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:41.290
I've been at show floors
where tumbleweeds could

00:05:41.290 --> 00:05:42.560
go blowing down the aisle--

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:44.650
I'm not kidding-- because
it's not worth going.

00:05:44.650 --> 00:05:47.650
And here there is so much
happening in this space

00:05:47.650 --> 00:05:51.190
right now that everywhere you
look there are people having

00:05:51.190 --> 00:05:52.540
interesting conversations.

00:05:52.540 --> 00:05:55.990
And this isn't something
where all the vendors are just

00:05:55.990 --> 00:05:58.574
scrabbling trying to get someone
to come listen to their sales

00:05:58.574 --> 00:05:59.073
pitch.

00:05:59.073 --> 00:06:00.910
It's where people are
coming here to learn.

00:06:00.910 --> 00:06:02.364
The sessions are fantastic.

00:06:02.364 --> 00:06:04.780
But everything that's going
on in this space is fantastic.

00:06:04.780 --> 00:06:06.239
So, of course, it's
getting bigger.

00:06:06.239 --> 00:06:08.238
Everyone's been saying,
oh, it's so much bigger.

00:06:08.238 --> 00:06:08.920
Of course.

00:06:08.920 --> 00:06:10.630
It's growing exponentially
it seems like.

00:06:10.630 --> 00:06:12.921
SAAD ALI: Yeah, we were
talking to Louie earlier today,

00:06:12.921 --> 00:06:15.850
and he mentioned that he
hasn't had a chance to attend

00:06:15.850 --> 00:06:17.860
any of the talks
because everybody's just

00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:20.140
so interested in talking
to him about Istio.

00:06:20.140 --> 00:06:22.529
DAN CIRULI: The thing
is that all of the talks

00:06:22.529 --> 00:06:23.570
are going to be recorded.

00:06:23.570 --> 00:06:24.778
You can watch all that later.

00:06:24.778 --> 00:06:28.450
What you can't do is go talk
to her and him and that person

00:06:28.450 --> 00:06:29.490
right there later.

00:06:29.490 --> 00:06:31.150
And so, yes, I
think a lot of us--

00:06:31.150 --> 00:06:33.760
I have been talking to
people nonstop today,

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:35.260
and I'll catch up
on sessions later.

00:06:35.260 --> 00:06:36.500
SAAD ALI: That's awesome.

00:06:36.500 --> 00:06:38.350
And speaking of sessions,
is there anything

00:06:38.350 --> 00:06:41.475
that you're interested
in watching later?

00:06:41.475 --> 00:06:44.050
DAN CIRULI: There are a
bunch of Istio sessions

00:06:44.050 --> 00:06:45.940
I'm going to watch.

00:06:45.940 --> 00:06:49.360
Envoy also-- Istio's
getting a lot of attention.

00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:52.450
Part of that is because
we did choose Envoy,

00:06:52.450 --> 00:06:55.240
and there is so much
happening in the Envoy space

00:06:55.240 --> 00:06:56.230
just on its own.

00:06:56.230 --> 00:06:59.160
And I'm certainly going to go
back and catch some of that.

00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:00.610
SAAD ALI: Awesome.

00:07:00.610 --> 00:07:04.000
And last question, what is
your favorite KubeCon memory?

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:08.890
DAN CIRULI: My favorite
KubeCon memory.

00:07:08.890 --> 00:07:13.160
That's a really,
really tough one.

00:07:13.160 --> 00:07:16.705
I'm going to have to say
Kelsey Hightower keynotes.

00:07:16.705 --> 00:07:17.710
SAAD ALI: Oh, yes.

00:07:17.710 --> 00:07:20.560
DAN CIRULI: And it's hard
to pick a particular one

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:22.450
because they're always amazing.

00:07:22.450 --> 00:07:25.510
Last year-- here's a
completely non-technical thing.

00:07:25.510 --> 00:07:28.120
Kelsey Hightower did his
entrance three times.

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:29.575
He came out, he
did his entrance,

00:07:29.575 --> 00:07:30.700
he's like what's happening.

00:07:30.700 --> 00:07:32.152
That's not enough.

00:07:32.152 --> 00:07:33.610
I'm going to go
back do that again.

00:07:33.610 --> 00:07:35.080
And he came out, and, of
course, then the crowd's

00:07:35.080 --> 00:07:35.704
really yelling.

00:07:35.704 --> 00:07:37.820
He was like that's not enough.

00:07:37.820 --> 00:07:40.100
And the third time people
are practically on feet.

00:07:40.100 --> 00:07:42.769
It was a very Kelsey
moment, and I loved it.

00:07:42.769 --> 00:07:43.810
SAAD ALI: We love Kelsey.

00:07:43.810 --> 00:07:45.292
Well, thank you very much, Dan.

00:07:45.292 --> 00:07:46.750
And enjoy the rest
of your KubeCon.

00:07:46.750 --> 00:07:46.990
DAN CIRULI: Had a blast.

00:07:46.990 --> 00:07:47.290
Thanks, Saad.

00:07:47.290 --> 00:07:48.340
SAAD ALI: Take care.

00:07:48.340 --> 00:07:50.790
